00:24:32.809 --> 00:24:45.690
Here is what's going to happen, Dimples.

00:24:45.690 --> 00:24:49.690
You are going to reach into that handy little tackle box of yours and pull us out a miracle.

00:24:49.690 --> 00:24:51.690
Oliver Queen does not die today.

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:56.960
The best I can do is keep them all up a few minutes longer.

00:24:56.960 --> 00:24:57.960
Then do it.

00:24:57.960 --> 00:25:39.519
I hate this bracelet to Megan for her birthday.

00:25:39.519 --> 00:25:49.059
birthday.

00:25:49.059 --> 00:25:53.059
She was joking around when she said that she'd wear it till the day that she died.

00:25:53.059 --> 00:26:06.509
There you go.

00:26:06.509 --> 00:26:18.130
Sorry.

00:26:18.130 --> 00:26:34.680
I swore that if I ever made it off this island, I'd change.

00:26:34.680 --> 00:26:45.839
I wouldn't waste my life.

00:26:45.839 --> 00:26:46.839
It's start to now.

00:26:46.839 --> 00:26:56.039
Well, it's ready.

00:26:56.039 --> 00:27:15.279
Let's go.

00:27:15.279 --> 00:27:16.279
Careful.

00:27:16.279 --> 00:27:17.279
That flower's poisonous.

00:27:17.279 --> 00:27:47.039
On the leash pond.

00:27:47.039 --> 00:27:49.039
Make some new friends.

00:27:49.039 --> 00:28:13.509
Just hit 106. He's on fire.

00:28:13.509 --> 00:28:15.509
It's just like Adriana.

00:28:15.509 --> 00:28:16.509
We need more ice and towels.

00:28:16.509 --> 00:28:31.839
In the back.

00:28:31.839 --> 00:28:37.869
More of this.

00:28:37.869 --> 00:28:42.069
Out of the way.

00:28:42.069 --> 00:28:50.210
You need you to breathe for me.

00:28:50.210 --> 00:28:51.210
Come on, Holly, stay with me.

00:28:51.210 --> 00:28:55.200
Clark, he's dying.

00:28:55.200 --> 00:28:56.200
He's it.

00:28:56.200 --> 00:29:36.329
No, it doesn't exist.

00:29:36.329 --> 00:29:44.680
Queen Industries, they have a lab nearby.

00:29:44.680 --> 00:29:45.680
You have to go find Mercer.

00:29:45.680 --> 00:29:46.680
What?

00:29:46.680 --> 00:29:48.680
Tess Mercer, Clark, please.

00:29:48.680 --> 00:29:50.680
Whoever did this to me, they're gonna go after her next.

00:29:50.680 --> 00:29:52.680
You know Tess.

00:29:52.680 --> 00:29:53.680
There's no time, Clark.

00:29:53.680 --> 00:29:55.680
She's working late. She's at the Daily Planet.

00:29:55.680 --> 00:30:41.309
Not an easy person to strike down, are you?

00:30:41.309 --> 00:30:44.309
There's a sketch little girl I remember.

00:30:44.309 --> 00:30:49.309
Remember, you haven't changed a bit.

00:30:49.309 --> 00:30:54.029
I think you'd be surprised.

00:30:54.029 --> 00:30:59.660
I meant to write you a postcard to say thank you.

00:30:59.660 --> 00:31:04.660
You were one of the men in my life that taught me how to fight back.

00:31:04.660 --> 00:32:05.049
Really?

00:32:05.049 --> 00:32:09.049
Whatever happened to the party boy hanging up his beer bong

00:32:09.049 --> 00:32:11.049
so that he could do good for the world?

00:32:11.049 --> 00:32:13.049
Well, I'm not the guy in that article anymore.

00:32:13.049 --> 00:32:14.049
Of course you're not.

00:32:14.049 --> 00:32:17.049
You're Oliver Queen, world-famous Samaritan.

00:32:17.049 --> 00:32:21.430
And I was his first save.

00:32:21.430 --> 00:32:38.160
Excuse me, Yvonne. You know you were much more than that.

00:32:38.160 --> 00:32:44.410
I'm glad you're okay, Oliver.

00:32:44.410 --> 00:32:46.410
After all, I need the Chief of Queen Industries alive and well

00:32:46.410 --> 00:32:49.410
if Luther Corp is going to keep its competitive edge.

00:32:49.410 --> 00:32:52.140
I'm happy to help.

00:32:52.140 --> 00:32:54.640
This is great.

00:32:54.640 --> 00:33:01.849
A few pay grades up from your days studying jellyfish and protesting whalerships, huh?

00:33:01.849 --> 00:33:06.849
You're not the first person to wonder how I arrived at the helm of the Luther dynasty.

00:33:06.849 --> 00:33:07.849
Well, what's to wonder?

00:33:07.849 --> 00:33:12.160
It's a wonder. You play to win.

00:33:12.160 --> 00:33:14.160
I found out how ruthless you were a long time ago.

00:33:14.160 --> 00:33:20.119
After what you did to me, did you really expect me to just sit around and eat ice cream?

00:33:20.119 --> 00:33:21.119
No.

00:33:21.119 --> 00:33:25.119
You'd rather work for my greatest enemy and then, just when I think the knife can't twist any farther.

00:33:25.119 --> 00:33:28.119
You take over his entire company.

00:33:28.119 --> 00:33:32.119
Oh, so that's what you do when you're not writing checks and grinning for the cameras.

00:33:32.119 --> 00:33:36.119
You sit around pondering ways that I've dedicated my life to your downfall.

00:33:36.119 --> 00:33:40.119
Sorry to break it to you, Oliver, but everything I did I did for me.

00:33:40.119 --> 00:33:41.119
I did for me.

00:33:41.119 --> 00:33:45.099
Then I guess our past is behind us.

00:33:45.099 --> 00:33:49.509
It has been since the day that you said goodbye.

00:33:49.509 --> 00:33:50.509
Oh, wait.

00:33:50.509 --> 00:33:55.359
You never actually did, did you?

00:33:55.359 --> 00:33:57.359
Is that why you called me here?

00:33:57.359 --> 00:33:58.359
Hmm?

00:33:58.359 --> 00:34:00.359
Because it's on the tip of my tongue.

00:34:00.359 --> 00:34:08.400
I called you to give you this.

00:34:08.400 --> 00:34:11.400
I realized that Lionel Luther is dead and buried,

00:34:11.400 --> 00:34:38.559
but maybe this will help you sleep at night.

00:34:38.559 --> 00:34:39.559
Lois.

00:34:39.559 --> 00:34:40.559
Hey, thanks, Smallville.

00:34:40.559 --> 00:34:42.559
Starting to get the hang of things around here.

00:34:42.559 --> 00:34:44.559
I was...

00:34:44.559 --> 00:34:45.559
I spoke to Chloe.

00:34:45.559 --> 00:34:46.559
She said you didn't come home last night.

00:34:46.559 --> 00:34:47.559
home last night.

00:34:47.559 --> 00:34:51.030
So not what you're thinking.

00:34:51.030 --> 00:34:52.030
I may have played a nurse with Oliver,

00:34:52.030 --> 00:34:54.030
but it never progressed to doctor.

00:34:54.030 --> 00:34:55.030
I've been working here all night.

00:34:55.030 --> 00:34:59.530
A kitten adoption fair this Sunday?

00:34:59.530 --> 00:35:02.530
You gave up your article in Oliver for this?

00:35:02.530 --> 00:35:07.679
Doesn't sound like you, Lois.

00:35:07.679 --> 00:35:10.679
Is it possible you're more shaken up about last night

00:35:10.679 --> 00:35:12.679
than you realized?

00:35:12.679 --> 00:35:14.679
You think?

00:35:14.679 --> 00:35:17.679
I was practically a three-shot martine.

00:35:17.679 --> 00:35:20.679
Yeah, but it doesn't mean you have to bury your motions in your work.

00:35:20.679 --> 00:35:25.679
This will be the most heart-freaking kitten adoption store you've ever read.

00:35:25.679 --> 00:35:30.400
What am I doing? Allie and I are through.

00:35:30.400 --> 00:35:31.400
Maybe.

00:35:31.400 --> 00:35:35.400
But just because the relationship's over doesn't mean the feelings go away.

00:35:35.400 --> 00:35:39.400
I'd be lying if I said I didn't watch the goodbye tape that Lana sent me more than once.

00:35:39.400 --> 00:35:43.400
Well, I'd rather avoid a ride on the Clark and Lana roller coaster.

00:35:43.400 --> 00:35:45.400
No offense.

00:35:45.400 --> 00:35:47.400
It's okay.

00:35:47.400 --> 00:35:50.400
I've got a delicate enough ego as it is.

00:35:50.400 --> 00:35:52.400
I am not cut out to date a guy with a hero complex.

00:35:52.400 --> 00:35:55.840
I mean, you were there.

00:35:55.840 --> 00:35:57.840
You saw him at the fundraiser.

00:35:57.840 --> 00:35:58.840
He's practically an icon, the man of tomorrow,

00:35:58.840 --> 00:36:01.840
and I'm just Lois Lane, the girl who writes about it.

00:36:01.840 --> 00:36:07.130
Well, there's nothing wrong with that.

00:36:07.130 --> 00:36:08.130
Right.

00:36:08.130 --> 00:36:11.130
So, when the occasional feelings flare up,

00:36:11.130 --> 00:36:13.130
you just got to trust your gut as hard as it is

00:36:13.130 --> 00:36:16.130
and realize that you broke up for a reason.

00:36:16.130 --> 00:36:18.130
Exactly.

00:36:18.130 --> 00:36:22.829
That one's in the house, Smallville.

00:36:22.829 --> 00:36:23.829
You should know.

00:36:23.829 --> 00:36:25.829
I'm allergic to cats.

00:36:25.829 --> 00:36:29.880
Get to work.

00:36:29.880 --> 00:36:49.500
Pop chop.

00:36:49.500 --> 00:36:51.500
I thought I closed those.

00:36:51.500 --> 00:36:53.500
I'm sorry they were open.

00:36:53.500 --> 00:36:55.500
What are you doing here?

00:36:55.500 --> 00:36:59.500
Well, I was checking in on Oliver's a hell of a close call last night.

00:36:59.500 --> 00:37:02.500
He's got a pretty great crew of friends.

00:37:02.500 --> 00:37:05.500
Renegade paramedic included.

00:37:05.500 --> 00:37:07.500
You must have done pretty well on your SATs.

00:37:07.500 --> 00:37:09.500
I thought you said you studied journalism.

00:37:09.500 --> 00:37:12.500
Well, yeah, journalism, and then there were the core classes

00:37:12.500 --> 00:37:14.500
and some, you know, electives and stuff.

00:37:14.500 --> 00:37:15.500
You're level with me, Chloe.

00:37:15.500 --> 00:37:18.500
You're some kind of genius. I'm embarrassing you.

00:37:18.500 --> 00:37:22.500
There's a reason I like to try and keep the doors closed.

00:37:22.500 --> 00:37:24.500
You know, there's nothing wrong with being smart.

00:37:24.500 --> 00:37:30.500
When the seating chart at your wedding looks more like a Euclidean algorithm than a dining hall, you know,

00:37:30.500 --> 00:37:32.500
you've got something seriously wrong.

00:37:32.500 --> 00:37:40.369
And the guy next to you on that seating chart, what does he think about all this?

00:37:40.369 --> 00:37:47.750
He doesn't know.

00:37:47.750 --> 00:37:49.750
Well, it's all sort of just happening.

00:37:49.750 --> 00:37:52.750
Which is why I would really prefer it if we could keep it between us.

00:37:52.750 --> 00:37:57.320
Well, Chloe, I think that in some weird way,

00:37:57.320 --> 00:37:59.320
we all have some part of us.

00:37:59.320 --> 00:38:03.650
we hold underneath the surface.

00:38:03.650 --> 00:38:04.650
How about even I do?

00:38:04.650 --> 00:38:21.860
So I hear I'm not the only one in town who owes you a drink.

00:38:21.860 --> 00:38:24.860
Tess Merser's attacker was arrested last night.

00:38:24.860 --> 00:38:28.750
Why didn't you tell me you knew Tess Merser before?

00:38:28.750 --> 00:38:33.119
It was in the past.

00:38:33.119 --> 00:38:36.119
Not exactly proud of the way things turned out.

00:38:36.119 --> 00:38:40.119
Doesn't change the fact that she knew the guy who tried to kill you.

00:38:40.119 --> 00:38:42.119
Yeah, well, it makes sense to me.

00:38:42.119 --> 00:38:44.119
The guy came after me.

00:38:44.119 --> 00:38:48.119
He's gonna go after her. She's the other captain of industry in town, so.

00:38:48.119 --> 00:38:49.119
Oliver, all that poised.

00:38:49.119 --> 00:38:51.119
Oliver, with all that poison in your system,

00:38:51.119 --> 00:38:53.119
are you sure adding more is the best idea right now?

00:38:53.119 --> 00:38:56.119
Yeah, Clark, why don't you stick to your milkshakes, huh?

00:38:56.119 --> 00:38:57.119
I'll know when I've had enough.

00:38:57.119 --> 00:39:07.760
Lionel Luther killed my parents.

00:39:07.760 --> 00:39:11.760
You figured that out from flight records?

00:39:11.760 --> 00:39:13.760
I mean, it was a plane crash.

00:39:13.760 --> 00:39:16.760
Yeah, it was a plane crash, and according to that,

00:39:16.760 --> 00:39:19.760
there were Luther Corp employees on the maintenance lock.

00:39:19.760 --> 00:39:29.440
And do you think Lionel paid them to sabotage the plane?

00:39:29.440 --> 00:39:30.440
Did you know about this?

00:39:30.440 --> 00:39:37.340
You were the son Lionel never had.

00:39:37.340 --> 00:39:38.340
The guy knew everything about you, right?

00:39:38.340 --> 00:39:39.340
So no.

00:39:39.340 --> 00:39:42.340
there and tell me that you didn't know a couple of his dirty little secrets.

00:39:42.340 --> 00:39:56.380
Look, Oliver, I was afraid that you'd do something you'd regret.

00:39:56.380 --> 00:39:58.380
Something I regret.

00:39:58.380 --> 00:40:01.380
Like what, Clark? You thought I was gonna kill him? Is that it?

00:40:01.380 --> 00:40:03.380
I wouldn't have killed him.

00:40:03.380 --> 00:40:09.269
I survived for two years on an island by myself.

00:40:09.269 --> 00:40:12.269
Clark, when it wasn't pouring rain, it was blistering sun.

00:40:12.269 --> 00:40:14.269
There were mosquitoes that ain't me alive.

00:40:14.269 --> 00:40:21.300
It's okay because you know what, it made me strong.

00:40:21.300 --> 00:40:25.300
You may be invincible, because you are not fearless, are you?

00:40:25.300 --> 00:40:26.300
You are you?

00:40:26.300 --> 00:40:28.300
You're afraid to trust your friends.

00:40:28.300 --> 00:40:30.300
And you're afraid to face who you were really meant to be.

00:40:30.300 --> 00:40:35.559
You're afraid to everything.

00:40:35.559 --> 00:40:39.230
Maybe you haven't been put to the test yet.

00:40:39.230 --> 00:40:43.050
Maybe your island's still out there, Clark.

00:40:43.050 --> 00:40:53.369
We're going to the airport, right?

00:40:53.369 --> 00:41:10.480
Who do you think paid your bail?

00:41:10.480 --> 00:41:14.880
Jails is good for you.

00:41:14.880 --> 00:41:27.030
I think he's having a heart attack you might want to take him to the hospital.

00:41:27.030 --> 00:41:29.030
What am I, an ambulance?

00:41:29.030 --> 00:41:31.030
They'll know what to do with her.

